---
title: Design System
draft: false
permalink: /design-system/index.html
layout: base
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
colorRoles:
  - {token: bg, role: Page, use: 'The page itself.'}
  - {token: bg-accent, role: Raised surface, use: 'Cards, inline code, footnotes, popups and form fields.'}
  - {token: bg-accent-2, role: Divider, use: 'Heading lines, short rules and the rules in prose.'}
  - {token: rule, role: Table rule, use: 'Hairlines between table rows, warmer than the gray ramp.'}
  - {token: bg-head, role: Table head, use: 'The table head row, opaque so a sticky head hides the rows under it.'}
  - {token: text, role: Text, use: 'Body text.'}
  - {token: headline, role: Headline, use: 'Headings.'}
  - {token: text-accent, role: Strong secondary text, use: 'Labels, metadata and captions that still need 4.5:1.'}
  - {token: text-subdued, role: Quiet text, use: 'Table heads and caption icons.'}
  - {token: accent-orange, role: Accent, use: 'Link underlines, the logo mark, focus and form accents. The same in both themes.'}
  - {token: logo-icon, role: Logo mark, use: 'The mark in the header and breadcrumb.'}
  - {token: border-top, role: Page edge, use: 'The band along the top of every page.'}
uiIcons:
  - {file: arrow-left, use: 'Previous image in a gallery.'}
  - {file: arrow-right, use: 'Next image in a gallery.'}
  - {file: chev-down, use: 'The menu button.'}
  - {file: x, use: 'Clears the search field. Outline, like search.'}
  - {file: search, use: 'Site search. Outline, in Phosphor''s regular weight.'}
  - {file: heart, use: 'The footer credit.'}
  - {file: star, use: 'The button samples on this page.'}
  - {file: rss, use: 'The feed link in the footer.'}
customColors:
  - property: '--color-text'
  - property: '--color-text-accent'
  - property: '--color-bg'
  - property: '--color-bg-accent'
  - property: '--color-bg-accent-2'
  - property: '--color-accent-orange'
  - property: '--color-accent-blue'
  - property: '--color-accent-green'
customGradients:
  - property: '--gradient-slate'
  - property: '--gradient-conic'
  - property: '--gradient-stripes'
parts:
  - {id: typography, title: Typography}
  - {id: links, title: Links and buttons}
  - {id: color, title: Color}
  - {id: grid, title: Grid}
  - {id: spacing, title: Spacing}
  - {id: icons, title: Icons}
  - {id: patterns, title: Patterns}
  - {id: tokens, title: Tokens}
colorGroups:
  - heading: 'Base palette'
    description: 'Foundational theme tones. Composed into the semantic theme variables in variables.css.'
    prefix: 'base'
  - heading: 'Neutral shades'
    description: 'Gray scale generated from a single base in OKLCH space. Includes the standalone gray-250.'
    prefix: 'gray'
  - heading: 'Vibrant shades'
    description: 'Orange scale generated from a single base in OKLCH space.'
    prefix: 'orange'
  - heading: 'Accent colors'
    description: 'Each has a chroma-reduced subdued variant used in dark theme.'
    prefixes: ['red', 'blue', 'green']
---

<!-- Inspired by: https://stevenwoodson.com/blog/eleventy-style-guide-generator-step-by-step-guide-adding-to-an-existing-site/ -->

<article class="design-system | wrapper flow">
  <header class="intro | flow region">
    <h1 class="gradient-text-linear">{{ title }}</h1>
    <p>The type, color, spacing and parts this site is built from. Swatches and scales on this page are read from the design tokens, so they always match the CSS.</p>
    <nav aria-label="Sections">
      <ul class="cluster gutter-xs" role="list">
        {%- for p in parts %}
          <li><a href="#{{ p.id }}" class="button" data-small-button data-button-variant="tertiary">{{ p.title }}</a></li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">01</p>
    {# ⚠ tabindex="-1" on each section heading lets a jump link focus it: that is what turns on the smooth scroll (html:focus-within in reset.css) and moves keyboard focus to the section. #}
    <h2 id="typography" tabindex="-1">Typography</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="fonts | flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Fonts</h3>

    <ul class="fonts flow">
      {%- for font in designTokens.fonts | dtcgItems -%}
        <li class="font" data-font="{{ font.name | slugify }}" style="font-family: var(--font-{{ font.name | slugify }})">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-{{ font.name | slugify }})">{{ font.name }}</h4>
          <p>{{ font.description }}</p>
          <p><strong>Font Families</strong>: {{ font.value | join(', ') }}</p>
          <code>var(--font-{{ font.name | slugify }})</code>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <div class="full sizes | section">
    <section class="section__inner wrapper flow region">
      <h3 class="heading-line">Sizes</h3>

      <p>
        Fluid type and space scales were
        <a href="{{ designTokens.textSizes.$extensions.scaleGenerator }}">generated with Utopia</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="table-sizes" data-table-layout="fixed">
      <table class="sizes">
        <caption id="table-sizes">Type sizes, smallest to largest</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Token</th><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">Sample</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        {%- for size in designTokens.textSizes | dtcgItems -%}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              <h4>{{ size.name }}</h4>
            </th>
            <td>
              <span class="flow">
                <p>{{ size.min }}px to {{ size.max }}px</p>
                <code>var(--size-{{ size.name | slugify }})</code>
              </span>
            </td>
            <td class="sizes-example">
              <p style="font-size:var(--size-{{ size.name | slugify }})">{{ size.name }}</p>
            </td>
          </tr>
        {%- endfor -%}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="type-features | flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Type features</h3>
    <p>Typographic defaults from Bringhurst's <cite>The Elements of Typographic Style</cite> and Butterick's <cite>Practical Typography</cite>. Each pair shows the plain setting, then the site's.</p>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Old-style figures</h4>
      <p>Running text (<code>.prose</code>) sets old-style, proportional figures, which sit with lowercase letters. Tables, acronyms and code keep lining figures.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Plain</p>
          <p>Founded in 1994, the W3C made CSS 2.1 a recommendation in 2011. Code keeps its own figures: <code>--tracking-wide: 0.16ch</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <p>Founded in 1994, the <abbr title="World Wide Web Consortium">W3C</abbr> made CSS 2.1 a Recommendation in 2011. Code keeps its own figures: <code>--tracking-wide: 0.16ch</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Small caps</h4>
      <p>The <code>.small-caps</code> class, by hand, for the opening words of an article or a name. Letterspaced, and never faked in italic, which has no small caps.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Plain</p>
          <p>The opening words of an article, or a name such as Robert Bringhurst.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <p><span class="small-caps">The opening words</span> of an article, or a name such as <span class="small-caps">Robert Bringhurst</span>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Figure classes</h4>
      <p>Four classes for figures, used by hand. They combine with each other and with the running-text default, so <code>class="lining-nums tabular-nums"</code> keeps both. Samples are set in running text (<code>.prose</code>). Put <code>.fraction</code> on the fraction itself: in Source Sans it changes every digit, full stop, comma and parenthesis it covers. <code>.slashed-zero</code> brings lining figures with it, because the old-style zero has no slashed form.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Without, then with <code>.fraction</code></p>
          <p>Add 3/4 cup of water and 1/2 teaspoon of salt.</p>
          <p>Add <span class="fraction">3/4</span> cup of water and <span class="fraction">1/2</span> teaspoon of salt.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Without, then with <code>.slashed-zero</code></p>
          <p>Serial number O0O-2020-0O.</p>
          <p class="slashed-zero">Serial number O0O-2020-0O.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Without, then with <code>.lining-nums</code></p>
          <p>The 1990s, the 2020s, and 1,380 metres.</p>
          <p class="lining-nums">The 1990s, the 2020s, and 1,380 metres.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Without, then with <code>.tabular-nums</code></p>
          <p>1,111.11<br />8,888.88</p>
          <p class="tabular-nums">1,111.11<br />8,888.88</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Captions and quotes</h4>
      <p>Quotes are balanced, so their lines come out even. Captions are upright and left-aligned, and set <code>pretty</code> so the last line never holds one word alone. The samples are held to a fixed measure so the difference shows at any screen width.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Plain</p>
          <figure><figcaption style="text-wrap: wrap; max-inline-size: 31ch">A caption long enough to run onto a second line under its picture, where the last word used to sit on its own.</figcaption></figure>
          <blockquote style="text-wrap: wrap; max-inline-size: 22ch">Simple is almost always more effective than complex, and short.</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <figure><figcaption style="max-inline-size: 31ch">A caption long enough to run onto a second line under its picture, where the last word used to sit on its own.</figcaption></figure>
          <blockquote style="max-inline-size: 22ch">Simple is almost always more effective than complex, and short.</blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Capital labels</h4>
      <p>Strings of capitals are letterspaced 5–10% of the size: <code>--tracking-wide</code> is 0.16ch, about 8%.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Before (0.12ch)</p>
          <p class="type-caps" style="letter-spacing: 0.12ch">Articles · Notes · Photos</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <p class="type-caps">Articles · Notes · Photos</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>Footnote numbers</h4>
      <p>The font's own superscript figures, bare, rather than a shrunken, raised number in brackets. They don't push the lines apart.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Plain</p>
          <p>A claim that needs a source.<sup>[1]</sup> The next line keeps its spacing, or doesn't.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <p>A claim that needs a source.<sup class="footnote-ref">1</sup> The next line keeps its spacing, or doesn't.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      <h4>List markers</h4>
      <p>List numbers take old-style figures, like the text around them; left alone, the browser sets them lining. They are tabular, so 9 and 10 line up. Numbers and hollow bullets share <code>--color-accent-orange-text</code>: <code>orange-600</code> in light mode, so the numbers pass 4.5:1, and <code>orange-500</code> in dark.</p>
      <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow" data-sample="plain">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Plain</p>
          <ol start="8">
            <li>Old-style figures in running text</li>
            <li>Lining figures with capitals</li>
            <li>Tabular figures in columns</li>
          </ol>
          <ul>
            <li>A bullet should be noticeable</li>
            <li>but not big</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="type-sample prose | flow">
          <p class="type-sample-label">Site</p>
          <ol start="8">
            <li>Old-style figures in running text</li>
            <li>Lining figures with capitals</li>
            <li>Tabular figures in columns</li>
          </ol>
          <ul>
            <li>A bullet should be noticeable</li>
            <li>but not big</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="blockquotes | flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Blockquotes</h3>
    <p>A quotation set off from the text: a rule on the start edge, a larger size, balanced lines, and no quote marks. The source goes inside the quote as its last paragraph, which the site sets two steps smaller.</p>

    <div class="grid" data-layout="50-50">
      <div class="prose | flow">
        <p class="type-sample-label">With a source</p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>Typography exists to honor content.</p>
          <p>— <span class="small-caps">Robert Bringhurst</span>, <cite>The Elements of Typographic Style</cite></p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p class="type-sample-label">Written in markdown</p>
        <pre><code>&gt; Typography exists to honor content.
&gt;
&gt; — &lt;span class="small-caps"&gt;Robert Bringhurst&lt;/span&gt;, *The Elements of Typographic Style*</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="rhythm | flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Vertical rhythm</h3>
    <p>Prose, where every gap, and every line of text that is not body text, is a whole number of quarter lines of body text (<code>global/blocks/prose-rhythm.css</code>). The wiki page <a href="/wiki/vertical-rhythm/">Vertical rhythm</a> explains and measures it. <kbd><kbd data-pc="Alt">⌥</kbd><kbd>R</kbd></kbd> draws the same lines over any page, counted from the top of the page.</p>

    <div class="rhythm-demo | flow">
      <label class="rhythm-toggle"><input type="checkbox"> Show the rhythm lines: whole lines strong, quarter lines faint</label>
      <div class="rhythm-sample | wrapper flow prose">
        <div class="wrapper-pass | flow">
          {% include "partials/rhythm-sample.njk" %}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">02</p>
    <h2 id="links" tabindex="-1">Links and buttons</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="flow region region-space-m">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Buttons</h3>
    <ul class="cluster" role="list">
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button">Default button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-button-variant="primary">primary button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-button-variant="secondary">secondary button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-button-variant="tertiary">tertiary button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-ghost-button>Ghost button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-button-radius="hard">Hard edges</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button"> {% svg "misc/star" %} Button with icon </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-small-button>small button</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#" class="button" data-icon><span class="visually-hidden">Icon button</span>{% svg "misc/star" %}</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">03</p>
    <h2 id="color" tabindex="-1">Color</h2>
  </header>
  <div class="full colors | section">
    <section class="section__inner wrapper flow region" aria-labelledby="color">
      <h3 class="heading-line">Roles</h3>
      <p>Every color has a job before it has a value. The page names the job; the theme picks the value. Values come from <code>semanticColors.json</code>.</p>
      <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="table-roles">
      <table class="color-roles">
        <caption id="table-roles">Color roles, in light and dark mode</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">Light</th><th scope="col">Dark</th><th scope="col">Used for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        {%- for r in colorRoles %}
          {%- set light = designTokens.semanticColors.themes.light[r.token] | colorRef(designTokens.colors) %}
          {%- set dark = designTokens.semanticColors.themes.dark[r.token] | colorRef(designTokens.colors) %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{{ r.role }}<br /><code>--color-{{ r.token }}</code></th>
            <td><span class="role-swatch" style="background-color: {{ light }}"></span><code>{{ light | lower }}</code></td>
            <td><span class="role-swatch" style="background-color: {{ dark }}"></span><code>{{ dark | lower }}</code></td>
            <td>{{ r.use }}</td>
          </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="heading-line | mt-l-xl">Palette</h3>

      {%- for group in colorGroups -%}
        {%- if group.description -%}<p{% if not loop.first %} class="mt-l-xl"{% endif %}>{{ group.description }}</p>{%- endif -%}
        <ul role="list">
          {%- for color in designTokens.colors | dtcgItems -%}
            {%- set slug = color.name | slugify -%}
            {%- set bracket = slug.split('-')[0] -%}
            {%- if (group.prefix and bracket == group.prefix) or (group.prefixes and bracket in group.prefixes) -%}
              <li>
                <div style="background-color: var(--color-{{ slug }})"></div>
                <p>
                  <code
                    >#{{ color.value | slugify }}<br />
                    var(--color-{{ slug }})</code
                  >
                </p>
              </li>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      {%- endfor -%}

      <h3 class="heading-line | mt-l-xl">Custom colors</h3>
      <p>in <code>variables.css</code>. Some only change for dark theme.</p>

      <ul role="list">
        {%- for color in customColors -%}
          <li>
            <div style="background-color: var({{ color.property }})"></div>
            <p>
              <code>{{ color.property }}</code>
            </p>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>

      <h3 class="heading-line | mt-l-xl">Gradients</h3>

      <ul role="list">
        {%- for gradient in customGradients -%}
          <li>
            <div style="background-image: var({{ gradient.property }})"></div>

            <p>
              <code>{{ gradient.property }}</code>
              <br />
              <!-- <code>{{ color.value }}</code> -->
            </p>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">04</p>
    <h2 id="grid" tabindex="-1">Grid</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Columns</h3>
    <p>Twelve columns of 6.25rem with 2rem gutters make the 97rem page; the middle eight make the 64rem column that prose sits in. As the window narrows, the outer columns go first, then the eight scale down with the text. <kbd><kbd data-pc="Alt">⌥</kbd><kbd>G</kbd></kbd> lays the grid over any page.</p>
    <figure class="grid-figure">
      <div class="grid-columns" aria-hidden="true">
        {%- for i in range(12) %}<span{% if i >= 2 and i < 10 %} data-prose{% endif %}></span>{% endfor %}
      </div>
      <figcaption>The twelve columns, with the eight of the prose column darker.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Breakouts</h3>
    <p>Anything on the page can step out of its column by a fixed amount: <code>.popout</code> by a gutter each side, <code>.feature</code> by a column and a gutter, <code>.full</code> to the window's edges. The bands below are drawn by those classes at their real widths, around the 64rem prose column, as in a post; widen the window to see each step.</p>
  </section>
  {# A full-bleed strip is a wrapper of its own here, at the prose column's 64rem, so the bands break out as they do in a post. #}
  <div class="breakout-demo | full wrapper prose-wrapper flow" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="breakout-band">content</div>
    <div class="breakout-band | popout">.popout</div>
    <div class="breakout-band | feature">.feature</div>
    <div class="breakout-band | full">.full</div>
  </div>

  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">05</p>
    <h2 id="spacing" tabindex="-1">Spacing</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="spacing | wrapper-pass flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Scale</h3>

    <p>
      Fluid type and space scales were
      <a href="{{ designTokens.spacing.$extensions.scaleGenerator }}">generated with Utopia</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrapper" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="table-spacing" data-table-layout="fixed">
    <table class="spacing">
      <caption id="table-spacing">Space sizes, smallest to largest</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Token</th><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">Sample</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      {%- for space in designTokens.spacing | dtcgItems -%}
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <h4>{{ space.name }}</h4>
          </th>
          <td>
            <span class="flow">
              <p>{{ space.min }}px to {{ space.max }}px</p>
              <code>var(--space-{{ space.name | slugify }})</code>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td>
            <div
              class="space"
              style="height:var(--space-{{ space.name | slugify }}); width:var(--space-{{ space.name | slugify }});"
            ></div>
          </td>
        </tr>
      {%- endfor -%}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">06</p>
    <h2 id="icons" tabindex="-1">Icons</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Post types</h3>
    <p>Icons are filled, to match the solid logo mark. They come from <a href="https://phosphoricons.com/">Phosphor</a>'s fill weight, drawn on a 256 grid and colored by <code>currentColor</code>, so an icon takes the color of the text beside it. Each file sits in <code>src/assets/svg/</code> under its old name, and <code>{% raw %}{% svg %}{% endraw %}</code> inlines it.</p>
    <ul class="icon-specimens | grid" role="list">
      {%- for t in navigation.postTypes %}
        <li>{% svg 'posts/' + t.icon %}<span>{{ t.text }}</span><code>{{ t.icon }}</code></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Interface</h3>
    <ul class="icon-specimens | grid" role="list">
      {%- for i in uiIcons %}
        <li>{% svg 'misc/' + i.file %}<span>{{ i.use }}</span><code>{{ i.file }}</code></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Platforms</h3>
    <p>Platform logos come from <a href="https://simpleicons.org/">Simple Icons</a>, each brand's own mark in one color. Phosphor has no mark for several of them, and two sets side by side in the footer would differ in size.</p>
    <ul class="icon-specimens | grid" role="list">
      {%- for key, url in personal.platforms %}
        <li>{% svg 'platform/' + key %}<code>{{ key }}</code></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">07</p>
    <h2 id="patterns" tabindex="-1">Patterns</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Meta line</h3>
    <p>Under every post title: the post type linked to its archive, the activity type for activities, the date, and the last revision linked on GitHub. Each item is an icon and a label that never break apart.</p>
    <p class="meta | cluster gutter-s-m">
      <a class="meta-item" href="/activities/">{% svg 'posts/activity', '', 'meta-icon' %}Activities</a>
      <span class="meta-item" data-activity="orienteering">{% svg 'posts/compass', '', 'meta-icon' %}Orienteering</span>
      <span class="meta-item">{% svg 'posts/calendar', '', 'meta-icon' %}7th of December, 2025</span>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Activity colors</h3>
    <p>Each activity type has one color, used for its map dots, its route lines and its icons. Orienteering changes shade so it is never as light as the hike green: the two are told apart by lightness alone under red–green color blindness. On a map it follows the map under it, so satellite takes the light orange and topographic the dark one.</p>
    <ul class="activity-swatches | cluster" role="list">
      {%- for a in ['orienteering', 'run', 'hike', 'weight-training'] %}
        <li data-activity="{{ a }}">{% svg "activity/" + a, "", "activity-type-icon" %}<span>{{ a | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</span><code>--color-activity-{{ a }}</code></li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Cards</h3>
    <p>Archive pages list posts as cards: one card per post type, each filling the same slots (image, date, headline, text, footer). These are the newest jam, book and note.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="card-samples | grid" data-layout="thirds">
    {%- set cardSamples = [
      {item: collections.jam | first, partial: 'card-jam.njk'},
      {item: collections.reading | first, partial: 'card-reading.njk'},
      {item: collections.note | first, partial: 'card-note.njk'}
    ] %}
    {%- for sample in cardSamples %}{% if sample.item %}
      {%- set item = sample.item %}{% set headingLevel = 'h4' %}{% set definedDate = item.date %}
      {% include "partials/" + sample.partial %}
    {%- endif %}{% endfor %}
  </div>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Maps</h3>
    <p>Maps are drawn from the site's own map tiles in its own colors, and load only when scrolled to. A map shows places (the <a href="/activities/">activities</a> overview), a single pin (a photo's location) or a recorded route. The wiki page <a href="/wiki/the-place-map/">The place map</a> covers how.</p>
    {%- set mapLat = 55.43287 %}{%- set mapLon = 13.93232 %}{%- set mapPlace = "Nybrostrand Beach, Skåne" %}
    <div style="--place-map-ratio: 2 / 1; --flow-space: var(--space-xl)">
      <place-map @lat="{{ mapLat }}" @lon="{{ mapLon }}" @zoom="13" @place="{{ mapPlace }}">
        {% include "partials/place-map.njk" %}
      </place-map>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Pamphlet</h3>
    <div>
      {%- set pamphlet = { svg: "covers/betanken-eder", label: "Cover of Betänken Eder! by Leo Tolstoy, Stockholm 1904" } %}
      {% include "partials/pamphlet.njk" %}
      <p>A cover can be shown as the object it belongs to. A stapled pamphlet lies on the table, floated into the text it sits in across three of the prose grid's eight columns; where the text would get too narrow it lies flat above it instead. It is built in CSS 3D around the cover, and settles face-on on hover.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="flow region">
    <h3 class="heading-line">Book</h3>
    <p>A hardcover stands with its pages showing. Like the pamphlet it is built in CSS 3D around the cover, and settles face-on on hover.</p>
    {%- set book = { src: "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/leo-tolstoy/the-kingdom-of-god-is-within-you/leo-wiener/downloads/cover.jpg", alt: "Cover of The Kingdom of God Is Within You by Leo Tolstoy, translated by Leo Wiener" } %}
    {% include "partials/book.njk" %}
  </section>

  <header class="part | flow region">
    <p class="part-number">08</p>
    <h2 id="tokens" tabindex="-1">Tokens</h2>
  </header>
  <section class="flow region">
    <p>Every value on this page starts in a <a href="{{ settings.repos.site }}/tree/main/src/_data/designTokens">design token file</a>, in the <a href="https://www.designtokens.org/">W3C design tokens format</a>. The build turns them into the CSS custom properties the site uses, token files for Penpot and Figma, and <code>DESIGN.md</code>, a written summary for people and tools.</p>
  </section>
</article>

{%- css "local" -%}
  {%- include 'css/design-system.css' -%}
  {%- include 'css/meta.css' -%}
  {%- include 'css/media-meta.css' -%}
  {%- include 'css/place-map.css' -%}
  {%- include 'css/place-map-overlay.css' -%}
  {%- include 'css/place-map-places.css' -%}
{%- endcss -%}
